# Payload default media upload directory
public/media/

# TypeScript incremental build cache (machine-specific, regenerated every build)
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
lateef-blog-batch.md

# Local MCP server config — holds a machine-specific credentials path; never commit
.mcp.json
# Google service-account keys (analytics-mcp etc.)
*-mcp.json
gcp-*.json
# Google OAuth client secrets
client_secret*.json
*.apps.googleusercontent.com.json

# Working reference material, not source: SEO/GSC audit exports and the brochure
# masters kept loose in the project root. Large binaries, and the exports carry
# Search Console data. Scoped deliberately rather than a blanket *.pdf — the
# old-website/ archive has six PDFs that ARE tracked, and a global rule would
# also silently swallow any genuine asset added under public/.
pdfs for seo/
/*.pdf

# pnpm artefacts. This project deploys with npm and tracks package-lock.json;
# a committed pnpm-lock.yaml would take precedence over it in the Railway
# build, silently switching production to a different package manager and a
# stale dependency snapshot. Some package.json scripts do use pnpm locally
# (reinstall, e2e:install), so these regenerate — they just must not be
# committed. pnpm-workspace.yaml here is only pnpm's unfilled allowBuilds stub.
pnpm-lock.yaml
pnpm-workspace.yaml
